{"name":"Ceiling Bright White","hex":"#e9ebe7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ceiling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The overhead closure of a room.","The upper limit of an object or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of ceil"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ceiling"},{"term":"bright","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Emitting much light; visually dazzling; luminous, lucent, radiant.","Of light: brilliant, intense."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Brightness, glow.\n\n Glory, splendour.","Brightness, glow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bright"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ceiling","blocks":[{"text":"A ceiling is an overhead interior roof that covers the upper limits of a room. It is not generally considered a structural element, but a finished surface concealing the underside of the roof structure or the floor of a story above. Ceilings can be decorated to taste, and there are many examples of frescoes and artwork on ceilings, especially within religious buildings. A ceiling can also be the upper limit of a tunnel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Ravenna%2C_basilica_di_San_Vitale_%28036%29.jpg/330px-Ravenna%2C_basilica_di_San_Vitale_%28036%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ceiling","wikidata":"Q221706"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Exeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG/500px-Exeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Exeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG/1280px-Exeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Exeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG/500px-Exeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AExeter_Cathedral_star_ceiling.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Paris%2C_Notre-Dame%2C_innen_2014-12_%283%29.jpg/500px-Paris%2C_Notre-Dame%2C_innen_2014-12_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Paris%2C_Notre-Dame%2C_innen_2014-12_%283%29.jpg/1280px-Paris%2C_Notre-Dame%2C_innen_2014-12_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Paris%2C_Notre-Dame%2C_innen_2014-12_%283%29.jp